<div class="exercise"><div class="ex-q"><p>Why does a smaller time step make the energy error shrink, but the chaotic trajectory still not “converge” over 30 s?</p>
</div><details><summary>Show the solution</summary><div class="ex-s"><p>RK4’s local error falls as <span class="katex"><span class="katex-html" aria-hidden="true"><span class="katex-base"><span class="katex-strut" style="height:0.8141em;"></span><span class="mord mathnormal">d</span><span class="mord"><span class="mord mathnormal">t</span><span class="msupsub"><span class="vlist-t"><span class="vlist-r"><span class="vlist" style="height:0.8141em;"><span style="top:-3.063em;margin-right:0.05em;"><span class="pstrut" style="height:2.7em;"></span><span class="katex-sizing reset-size6 size3 mtight"><span class="mord mtight">5</span></span></span></span></span></span></span></span></span></span></span>, so energy drift becomes tiny. But any error, even rounding at <span class="katex"><span class="katex-html" aria-hidden="true"><span class="katex-base"><span class="katex-strut" style="height:0.8141em;"></span><span class="mord">1</span><span class="mord"><span class="mord">0</span><span class="msupsub"><span class="vlist-t"><span class="vlist-r"><span class="vlist" style="height:0.8141em;"><span style="top:-3.063em;margin-right:0.05em;"><span class="pstrut" style="height:2.7em;"></span><span class="katex-sizing reset-size6 size3 mtight"><span class="mord mtight"><span class="mord mtight">−</span><span class="mord mtight">16</span></span></span></span></span></span></span></span></span></span></span></span>, is amplified exponentially by chaos; after enough seconds two step sizes give completely different trajectories, both conserving energy. You can trust statistics and invariants of a chaotic simulation, not its long-term path.</p>
</div></details></div>
